<section class="engineering-stack" aria-labelledby="engineering-stack-title">
  <header class="engineering-stack__header">
    <p class="lab-label">The deeper engineering lesson</p>
    <h2 id="engineering-stack-title">A secure device is a chain of executable truths</h2>
    <p>
      The wallet is the case study. The more general principle is that modern engineering
      connects a domain model, executable firmware, physical interaction, simulation, and
      evidence without letting any layer invent a different story.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="engineering-stack__thesis">
    <p>Human intent</p>
    <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <p>Physical input</p>
    <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <p>Firmware event</p>
    <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <p>Domain transition</p>
    <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <p>Electrical consequence</p>
  </div>

  <div class="engineering-stack__layers">
    {sources.map((source) => (
      <article>
        <span class="engineering-stack__number">{source.index}</span>
        <div>
          <p class="engineering-stack__project">{source.project}</p>
          <h3>{source.name}</h3>
          <p>{source.detail}</p>
        </div>
        <a href={source.href} aria-label={`Inspect ${source.project} source at ${source.commit}`}>
          <code>{source.commit}</code><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="engineering-stack__invariant">
    <p>One button press in the lab above crosses every boundary:</p>
    <code>SVG button → GPIO P14/P16 → Cortex-M instruction stream → wallet-core Event → Effect → display frame → NodeSpice load state</code>
  </div>

  <aside class="engineering-stack__boundary">
    <strong>Evidence boundary</strong>
    <p>
      The browser firmware is a teaching target that executes the real reducer on Firmverse’s
      PHY6252 Cortex-M0 backend. The repository’s full cryptographic surface remains measured
      separately as Cortex-M4/M33 firmware evidence. The electrical circuit remains a first-order
      model until component models and board measurements replace its explicit equivalents.
    </p>
  </aside>
</section>
